rculation (you have one, one was discarded), so 2 left \u2014 you have one, one out, so one left.\n\nSame for B3: you have one, one used in chow (from discard), so two out, two left \u2014 unshown: B3:3 \u2192 wait, unshown says B3:3, meaning 3 not revealed.\n\nYou have one B3 in hand, and one in meld (claimed), so two B3 revealed.\n\nTotal 4 copies \u2192 so one more in wall/hands.\n\nSimilarly, J2: you have one, you discarded one earlier \u2192 two revealed, unshown: J2:2 \u2192 so two still out there? Contradiction.\n\nUnshown tiles: J2:2 \u2192 means 2 copies not yet revealed.\n\nBut you discarded J2 in history, and now have J2 in hand \u2014 so at least two revealed.\n\nSo unshown should be 2 \u2192 ok, so two still unrevealed.\n\nTotal 4: two in unknown locations, two revealed (your discard and your current hand).\n\nSo one other player may have one.\n\nSo J2 not completely safe.\n\nBut since you played it before, and no one punged, probably not highly valued.\n\nSimilarly, B3: used in chow, so visible.\n\nNow, **strategic choice**:\n\nTo pursue **Seven Pairs** (24 points), keep all possible pairs and discard a singleton.\n\nBest discards: W7, B3, or J2.\n\nW7 is part of a potential W78 sequence \u2014 but you have no W6 or W5.\n\nAnd you already have a chow in bamboos.\n\nNot helpful.\n\nMoreover, W7 could complete someone\u2019s Upper Tiles or Full Flush.\n\nSo discarding W7 is risky.\n\nB3: completes [B1,B2,B3], [B2,B3,B4], [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 but [B2,B3,B4] is already formed by you, so others may have moved on.\n\nAlso, B1, B2, B4 have been played \u2014 so B3 less useful.\n\nSafer than W7.\n\nJ2: dragon, always somewhat useful, but only two copies, and one already discarded \u2014 so medium safety.\n\nBut in Seven Pairs, J2 pair is worth it.\n\nHowever, with four pairs already (W8, W9, T6, T8), adding any one of W7, B3, J2 as pair gives five \u2014 close.\n\nBut better to keep options.\n\nYet you must discard one.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nPreserve the strongest pattern: **Seven Pairs** requires minimizing loss of pair candidates.\n\nBut W7 is the only singleton not duplicated in hand.\n\nW8, W9, T6, T8 are pairs \u2014 do not discard.\n\nJ2 and B3: you have only one each \u2014 but J2 is honor, less likely to help in chows for others? No, dragons can be in pungs.\n\nBut since you already discarded J2, and no one Peng, likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, B3 was used in Chi by you \u2014 so opponent discarded it willingly.\n\nSo both B3 and J2 are relatively safe.\n\nBut **J2** is a dragon \u2014 discarding it might be safer than W7.\n\nAlso, **Dragon Pung** is only 2 points, not worth pursuing.\n\nSo discard **J2**.\n\nIt maintains your pair strength in suited tiles.\n\nAlternatively, discard **B3** \u2014 but B3 could help someone complete a chow.\n\nFor example, a player with B1,B2 might need B3.\n\nOr B4,B5.\n\nBut B4 has been played by two players \u2014 so possibly not safe.\n\nB3 might be more dangerous.\n\nW7: no one has discarded W7, W6, W8, W9 much \u2014 W8 and W9 in your hand.\n\nBut W7 could complete W789 for someone.\n\nRiskier.\n\nSo safest: **J2**\n\nAlso, honors are generally safer to discard late, but here early-mid game.\n\nBut you have no use for it.\n\n**Reason**: To pursue the high-value **Seven Pairs** (24 points) pattern, it is crucial to preserve all existing pairs (W8, W9, T6, T8). Among the singleton tiles (W7, B3, J2), J2 is relatively safer to discard because dragon tiles are less commonly used in chows, and its prior discard did not trigger a 'Peng', suggesting limited demand. Keeping W7 offers potential for character-suit combinations, while B3 could complete an opponent's chow.\n\nAnswer: Play J2"}
