high-scoring pattern\n\nAnd critically: **you already have a chow**, so **Peng doesn't unlock All Pungs**.\n\nMoreover, **Mixed Shifted Pungs** requires three pungs in different suits, consecutive numbers. T8 would need W7 and B9 or similar \u2014 unlikely.\n\nSo **low value in making this pung**.\n\nAlso, consider **tile safety and future draws**. T2 is a terminal, and you only have one \u2014 safe to discard. But if you pass now, you can choose what to discard on your turn.\n\nBy **Passing**, you retain control, avoid committing to a weak pung, and wait for better opportunities \u2014 perhaps draw a tile to improve hand shape.\n\nAdditionally, other players may be close to winning \u2014 the across player has strong melds (W7 pung, T7-T8-T9 chow), so game may end soon. But exposing a weak pung won't help you win faster.\n\nFinal consideration: **are there defensive implications?** Peng-ing T8 reveals information \u2014 shows you are collecting T8s, which might alert others. But since T8 is mid-number and not rare, probably negligible.\n\nConclusion: **Peng T8 offers minimal strategic benefit and commits you to a suboptimal structure. Passing preserves flexibility and avoids wasting a turn on a non-critical meld.**\n\nReason: The Peng action does not significantly advance the hand toward a high-scoring pattern, especially since the existing chow prevents \"All Pungs.\" The T8 pung has limited synergy with current tiles, and passing allows for better hand development through draws.\n\nAnswer: Pass"}
